{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n73 \n \n \nAppendix G. Crew Activity per Space Station Science Highlights  \nThe subsequent text and images are from the weekly highlights prepared by the Lead Increment Scientist \nand made available via e-mail and online via www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/.   \n \nWith the major exception of the Space Station Science Highlights, links were generally not included in the \nexcerpts herein because they typically go to secondary sources of information for the ACME project, for \nwhich the primary web site is https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/.  \nClarifications have been added in red. \n \n2019 \nFirst round (normal flames) \nWeek of April 1, 2019 \nOther investigations on which the crew performed work: \n\u2022 \nThe Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and \noxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in \nmicrogravity.   \n \nWeek of April 8, 2019 \nOther investigations on which the crew performed work: \n\u2022 \nThe Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) includes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and \noxidizer control, and five different cameras for performing combustion investigations in \nmicrogravity.   \n \n \nNASA astronaut Nick Hague replaced an igniter in the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiment \n(ACME), a set of six studies of gaseous flames conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). The CIR \nincludes an optics bench, combustion chamber, fuel and oxidizer control, and five different cameras for \nperforming combustion experiments in microgravity. \nCredits: NASA \nWhile Hague did support Flame Design operations, this photo is from April 3 work in support of ACME\u2019s \nBurning Rate Emulator (BRE) experiment.  That study\u2019s final test day was on April 4, while Christina Koch \nbegan Flame Design preparations on April 5.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 73}}